course: dictionary
lesson: 10114
title: Thistle
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you can see in nature. When you meet one, the words around it can tell you what it is like. «There a puppy, enormous to tiny Alice, bounds at her; she keeps it busy with a stick, dodging round a thistle, until it gets tired and she escapes.» Dodging round something means moving around it to avoid it, so the thistle is something Alice does not want to walk into.
explain: Why would she go around it? A thistle is a wild plant with prickly leaves and stems. Those prickles protect the plant, so the clue is that it is something you go round, not through.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «dodging round a prickly plant» still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
fact: A thistle is a wild plant with prickly leaves and stems.
fact: Its round flower head is usually purple, though it can be yellow or white.
fact: Thistle seeds float away on fluffy down, carried by the wind.
fact: The thistle is the national emblem of Scotland.
fact: Butterflies and goldfinches love thistles.
exercise: What is a thistle? | A kind of bird that sings at dawn | *A wild plant with prickly leaves and stems | A tall tree with smooth bark
exercise: «She keeps it busy with a stick, dodging round a thistle.» Why does Alice go round the thistle? | It is hidden under the water | It is a friendly animal she wants to pet | *It is prickly, so going round it is safer
exercise: Which animal loves thistles? | Crocodile | *Goldfinch | Polar bear
test: What colour is the round flower head of a thistle most often? | Blue | Black | *Purple | Green
test: What carries thistle seeds away on their fluffy down? | Its own roots | A busy bee | A river | *The wind
exam: Which country has the thistle as its national emblem? | England | *Scotland | Ireland | Wales
